Under DORA Article 30, financial entities must include in their ICT third-party service contracts clear written allocation of rights and obligations, with the full contract and service level agreements set out in one written document available in a durable, accessible format [1].

At a minimum, the contractual arrangements must include:
- a clear and complete description of the functions and ICT services to be provided, including whether subcontracting of critical or important functions is allowed and under what conditions [1];
- the locations where services and data processing take place, including storage location, and advance notice of any location changes [1];
- provisions on availability, authenticity, integrity and confidentiality of data protection, including personal data [1][2];
- access, recovery and return of personal and non-personal data in an easily accessible format if the provider becomes insolvent, is resolved, discontinues business, or the contract ends [2];
- service level descriptions, including updates and revisions [2];
- assistance to the financial entity at no additional cost, or at a pre-agreed cost, when an ICT incident related to the service occurs [2];
- cooperation with the competent authorities and resolution authorities, including persons appointed by them [2];
- termination rights and minimum notice periods, in line with competent and resolution authority expectations [2];
- conditions for the provider’s participation in ICT security awareness programmes and digital operational resilience training [2][3].

For services supporting critical or important functions, additional clauses are required:
- full service level descriptions with precise quantitative and qualitative performance targets for effective monitoring and corrective action [3];
- notice periods and reporting obligations, including notification of developments that may materially affect the provider’s ability to deliver the services [3];
- requirements to implement and test business contingency plans and maintain appropriate ICT security measures, tools and policies [3];
- detailed access, inspection and audit rights, including scope, procedures and frequency [4];
- exit strategies, including a mandatory adequate transition period so the provider continues services while the financial entity migrates to another provider or in-house solution [4].

The documents also say that, when negotiating these arrangements, financial entities and providers shall consider standard contractual clauses developed by public authorities for specific services [4].

The documents do not cover whether any additional contractual provisions are required outside Article 30, beyond these listed items, or provide model wording for the clauses [1][2][3][4].

The only deadline stated in the provided documents is in DORA Article 42(1): within 60 calendar days of receiving the Lead Overseer’s recommendations, a critical ICT third-party service provider must either notify the Lead Overseer of its intention to follow the recommendations or provide a reasoned explanation for not following them [1].

The documents also mention other deadlines, such as:
- urgent opinions or urgent binding decisions under GDPR Article 66(4), which must be adopted within two weeks [2];
- compliance dates under the AI Act, including 31 December 2030, 2 August 2030, and 2 August 2027 [4];
- and that the Lead Overseer must set a time limit when requesting information under DORA Article 37, but no specific number of days is given there [7].

If you meant a different deadline, the documents do not specify which one.
